# Team Knowledge

This file is loaded by the SRE Investigation Agent and prepended to the
conclusion prompt. Use it to encode team-specific facts that aren't
obvious from telemetry alone — known issues, ownership, false-positive
patterns, escalation paths, etc.

Copy this file to `team_knowledge.md` (without `.example`) in the project
root and edit it for your environment. It is loaded automatically when
present and silently ignored when missing.

Keep it concise — every line goes into the LLM prompt. Aim for under
~150 lines total. Use plain language; the model reads markdown well.

---

## Service Ownership

<!-- Map services to owning teams. Helps Claude attribute remediation
     steps to the right group. -->

- `service-a` — Platform team (#platform-oncall)
- `service-b` — Search team (#search-oncall)
- `database-x` — Data team (#data-oncall)

## Known Issues / Recurring Patterns

<!-- Things that have caused incidents before and might cause them again.
     If Claude sees a similar pattern, it can flag it. -->

- `service-a` has a known memory leak under sustained traffic above
  10K RPS. Pods OOM-restart roughly every 6 hours when traffic is high.
  Mitigation: HPA usually catches this; if not, manual restart.

- The Friday batch job in `pipeline-x` always emits a brief error spike
  between 02:00–02:15 UTC. **This is expected** and should not be
  flagged as an incident.

## False-Positive Monitors

<!-- Monitors that fire often but aren't real incidents. Claude should
     down-weight findings sourced primarily from these. -->

- Monitor 12345: synthetic browser test that fails on every deploy of
  `frontend-app` for ~3 minutes during pod rotation. Not a real outage.

- Monitor 67890: alerts on >0% error rate but the service has a baseline
  of 0.1% errors from health-check probes. Investigate only if rate >2%.

## Escalation Paths

<!-- Who to page for what. Helps Claude write more actionable
     remediation steps. -->

- Spanner / database issues → `#data-oncall`
- Authentication failures → `#identity-oncall`
- Search relevance / ranking issues → `#search-oncall`
- Frontend / mobile errors → `#client-oncall`

## Architectural Notes

<!-- Non-obvious facts about how services interact. Especially useful
     for cross-service investigations where the dependency isn't visible
     in the service map. -->

- `service-a` calls `service-b` via gRPC, but only for requests where
  the user has the `experimental` feature flag enabled. If a service-b
  outage affects only a fraction of service-a traffic, this is why.

- All writes to `database-x` go through `write-proxy` to enforce
  rate limits. If `write-proxy` is unhealthy, all writes fail even
  though the database itself is up.
